<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hintsa kaKhawuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1790–<a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hintsa kaKhawuta was king of the Gcaleka Xhosa, paramount among the Xhosa chiefs, from about 1820 to 1835. During the Sixth Frontier War the British accused him of harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, and he came to the camp of Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Benjamin D'Urban</a> to negotiate. Held as a hostage, he was shot dead while trying to escape in May 1835, and his body was mutilated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hintsa-kakhawuta.jpg" alt="Hintsa kaKhawuta" width="170" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anon. Cape Colony. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_Hintsa_of_the_Gcaleka_Xhosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hintsa, also known as Hintsa Zanzolo, was the son of Khawuta ka Gcaleka, eldest son of Gcaleka ka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalo">Phalo</a>, and of Nobutho, said to be a daughter of Tshatshu. He was the ninth descendant of King Tshawe, founder of the Xhosa Kingdom, and he is often compared with him. His capital at Gcuwa (Butterworth) was named Kulo Mali. Of his four known sons, Sarili ka Hintsa was born to his first wife, Nomsa kaGambushe Tshezi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hintsa was crowned in 1820, taking over from his uncle Nqoko ka Gcaleka, who had been regent. At its height under him the kingdom stretched from the Mbhashe River to the Gamtoos River. It was led by two houses, the ruling Gcaleka or Great House and the Rharhabe or right-hand house, and it faced civil wars between chiefs and invasions by refugee peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>. The Xhosa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thembu">Thembu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondomise">Mpondomise</a> combined to crush the Bhaca under Madzikane, killing him, and Hintsa later repulsed a Bhaca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondo">Mpondo</a> attack on the Bomvana.</p><p>The Sixth Frontier War, sometimes called the Hintsa War, began on 21 December 1834, when a large force of Rharhabe Xhosa under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maqoma">Maqoma</a> and Tyali swept into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. Hintsa gave them moral support but sent no army. In early February 1835 he offered the British 1,000 men against the Rharhabe chiefs but would not betray them, and Governor Benjamin d'Urban declared war on him. On 14 April 1835 d'Urban, holding him responsible for the attacks, announced the annexation of the land between the Keiskamma and the Great Kei as Queen Adelaide Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Harry Smith demanded 50,000 head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> in compensation for the war of 1834 and the surrender of Hintsa's chiefs, and Hintsa was held captive until the terms were met; he sent word to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maqoma">Maqoma</a> to prepare to defend the country. In Noël Mostert's account, on 12 May 1835 Hintsa broke away from the column on horseback, and George Southey of the Corps of Guides shot him in a river. The men took his ornaments and cut off his ears as relics, and Smith left the body lying on the ground.</p><p>Hintsa is regarded as a hero of South African and Xhosa history, remembered in oral poems and stories. In 1996 Nicholas Tilana Gcaleka claimed to have brought back his skull from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, but the Gcaleka Xhosa monarch, Xolilizwe Sigcawu, refused to sanction its burial, and forensic tests showed it was most likely the skull of a middle-aged European woman. The King Hintsa Bravery Award was established in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hintsa kaKhawuta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hintsa</b> was drawn into the Sixth Frontier War and was shot while held by British forces in 1835.</blockquote><a href="/state/gcaleka-xhosa-kingdom">Gcaleka Xhosa Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xhosa king <b>Hintsa</b> was shot while in British custody during the campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-queen-adelaide">Province of Queen Adelaide</a></li></ul></section>
